Average "Yu-Gi-Oh" fan speaking. I've been playing it since 2005 on a GBA. Didn't like the anime sereies though, but that's not what we're talking about today. Since it was my very first CCG I've quickly grown to love the game. And now there's finally a possibility not only to play single campaigns or using a mere local multiplayer on consoles but also to challenge other players all over the world. Much thanks for that!
Now I'd like to tell about what's good and bad in "Duel Links". Good things are:
+ The original gameplay mechanics, which we are all used to;
+ A wide variety of cards - the ones from the original series along with the new ones;
+ There are always special offers and events, which let you earn extra experience, coins or gems;
+ A nice reward system, which allows to collect pretty good cards without paying too much actual money;
+ Fair PvP rank matches - you're guaranteed to duel an opponent with the same skill level.
+ Pretty good treats for beginners with lots of quizzes and tutorials.
There was also a couple of things I didn't like too much. They are:
- The reduced game field and LP. Now we are able to have only a maximum of 3 monsters and 3 spell/trap cards on the field along with 4000 LP instead of normal 8000. As I started to play I didn't pay much attention to it, because these changes seemed not to influence the overall gameplay. But then I've realized that reduced life points also mean shorter duels. And this fact leads to a not very pleasant conclusion: your victory highly depends on which cards are drawn from your deck at the beginning of a duel. Here's some statistics: at the beginning you receive 4 or 5 cards (depending on who's going first); my average duel lasts 4-5 turns, and that means all you can get is 4 or 5 more cards; and there you have it - in my average duel only 8-10 cards out of 20 (minimal deck size) are being used. This makes building a well balanced deck rather hard, but luckily the game doesn't become unplayable because of that.
- The other thing is the complete abscence of a single campaign. Not a big deal. But it would be nice to have at least some mini-stories to go through. Because duelling with an NPC is rather boring, and I don't always have the mood to play PvP.
In conclusion I'd like to recommend the game anyway. It may have some issues but it's rather good and addictive both for experienced players and for beginners or CCG fans. I just hope Konami doesn't ruin their creation.

